version 0.9.2
schematic divider-network

# Two resistive dividers off one reference: a three-leg ladder with two taps, and a plain two-leg
# divider. The second divider is the same shape as the first with a different ratio -- and that
# ratio is not something the language can say. There is no expression (ADR-0001 D3) and no module
# parameter (F13), so the values and the tap voltages are literals the designer worked out by hand.
#
# The windows are therefore the designer's arithmetic, not the checker's: `voltage TAP1 2.2V..2.2V`
# is a statement about the tap, and the checker compares the parts against it -- it cannot check
# that the divider divides. Five resistors of one value make three different voltages here; the
# ratio lives in how the resistors are connected, and the description does not say so.
instances
  R1  IDnphqjf  res-thick-0402  100k  ±1%
  R2  IDpynvkp  res-thick-0402  100k  ±1%
  R3  IDts5dqz  res-thick-0402  100k  ±1%
  R4  ID4kxgem  res-thick-0402  100k  ±1%
  R5  ID4nq9r2  res-thick-0402  100k  ±1%

nets
  GND:   R3.2  R5.2
  TAP1:  R1.2  R2.1
  TAP2:  R2.2  R3.1
  TAP3:  R4.2  R5.1
  VREF:  R1.1  R4.1

ports
  GND   GND
  VREF  VREF

constraints
  voltage  GND   0V..0V
  voltage  VREF  3.3V..3.3V
  voltage  TAP1  2.2V..2.2V
  voltage  TAP2  1.1V..1.1V
  voltage  TAP3  1.65V..1.65V
